Hmm, Java has: `if (c == ']') { if (first != 0 || ...) break; else throw?` I don't remember exactly. Dart: `[!]` → `[^]` — wait: i=start+1, glob[i]=='!' → writes '[^', i++; loop: glob[i]==']' → exits loop, writes ']' → regex `[^]`. In Dart regex, `[^]` is... `[^]` is INVALID regex? In Dart, `RegExp('[^]')` — an empty negated class. Dart regexp (based on RE2?) — Dart uses its own regexp engine (Irregexp, like V8). In V8, `[^]` is VALID (matches any character, like dot). In V8/JS, `[]` and `[^]` are valid (empty classes match nothing/anything). So `[!]` → `[^]` matches any char. Java would throw → pattern skipped. Divergence again — same trusted-config bucket. Fold into the grammar-divergences suggestion.

**Issue candidate 4 — `RegExp.escape(glob[i])` per-character inside char class for `-`:** documented. Ranges preserved. But what about a `-` that RegExp.escape... I confirmed RegExp.escape doesn't escape `-`. OK.

**Issue candidate 5 — performance of canonicalizePath on every blocked-path candidate:** only on containment failure. Fine.

**Issue candidate 6 — `isAllowedByConfig(resolved, base, configuredAllowedPaths)` — note `base` is the raw base (possibly non-canonical). matchesPattern canonicalizes via _patternBase + canonicalizePath. ✓

**Issue candidate 7 — `_sandboxReadPath` on the bridge reads PropertyReader per call — includes reading dmtools.env from disk (cached? "File loading (lazy, cached)"). Let me verify caching exists to avoid a per-call disk hit on every file op. The doc says "(lazy, cached)" — let me confirm.

