The Java source matches the Dart port quite closely. Let me note some subtle differences:

1. Java `matchesPattern`: `PathMatcher matcher = FileSystems.getDefault().getPathMatcher("glob:" + globSuffix); return matcher.matches(relative);` — Java's glob `getPathMatcher().matches()` requires the ENTIRE path to match (it's a full match), which the Dart code does with `^...$` anchors. Good.

2. Java glob semantics (sun.nio.fs.Globs):
   - `*` matches zero or more characters of a name segment WITHOUT crossing directory boundaries → `[^/]*`. ✓ matches Dart.
   - `**` matches characters crossing directory boundaries → `.*`. ✓
   - `?` one character without crossing boundary → `[^/]`. ✓
   - `[...]` character class. In Java globs, `[!...]` negates. ✓
   - `{...}` alternation. ✓
   - In Java globs, `\` escapes... Actually in Java glob grammar, `\` escapes the next character ("Within [...] the \ is used literally"?). Let me recall sun.nio.fs.Globs:
     - `The \ character is used to escape any of the characters `*?[{`\` ... otherwise interpreted specially.` Actually from the javadoc of getPathMatcher:
       ```
       The following rules are used to interpret glob patterns:
       - The * character matches zero or more characters of a name component without crossing directory boundaries.
       - The ** characters matches zero or more characters crossing directory boundaries.
       - The ? character matches exactly one character of a name component.
       - The backslash character (\) is used to escape characters that would otherwise be interpreted as special characters.
       - The [ ] characters are a character class ...
       - The { } characters are a group of subpatterns, ...
       - All other characters match themselves.
       ```
     Also: "When a path segment name is matched against a glob pattern, a leading dot is treated like a regular character"? No that's not relevant here.

     In Java's sun.nio.fs.Globs implementation:
     - In char class `[...]`, `!` at start negates; `-` for ranges; `\` escapes... let me check: In Globs regex generation, within `[...]`, characters are quoted unless `-`. Actually sun.nio.fs.Globs toRegexPattern:
       ```java
       case '[':
           if (nextIsEscaped?)...
           // hmm
       ```
     Let me look at the actual code:
     ```java
     private static String toRegexPattern(String globPattern) {
         boolean inGroup = false;
         StringBuilder regex = new StringBuilder("^");
         int i = 0;
         while (i < globPattern.length()) {
             char c = globPattern.charAt(i++);
             switch (c) {
                 case '\\':
                 case '/':
                 case '$':
                 case '^':
                 case '+':
                 case '.':
                 case '(':
                 case ')':
                 case '=':
                 case '!':
                 case '|':
                     regex.append('\\').append(c);
                     break;
                 case '?':
                     regex.append("[^/]");
                     break;
                 case '[':
                     if (inGroup) regex.append("\\[");
                     else {
                         boolean hasRangeStart = false;
                         boolean hasRangeEnd = false;
                         boolean isRange = false;
                         boolean negated = false;
                         if (i < globPattern.length() && globPattern.charAt(i) == '!') {
                             negated = true;
                             i++;
                         }
                         regex.append('[');
                         if (negated) regex.append('^');
                         char first = 0;
                         while (i < globPattern.length()) {
                             c = globPattern.charAt(i++);
                             if (c == ']') {
                                 if (first != 0) { // hmm actually there's a check
                                     throw new PatternSyntaxException("Missing end");
                                 }
                                 break;
                             }
                             ...
                             regex.append(c) ... with range handling
                         }
                     }
                     ...
                 case '{':
                     if (inGroup) throw ...; regex.append("(?:(?:"); inGroup = true; break;
                 case '}':
                     ... regex.append("))"); inGroup = false; break;
                 case ',':
                     if (inGroup) regex.append(")|(?:(?:"); else regex.append(','); break;
                 case '*':
                     if (globPattern.length() >= i && globPattern.charAt(i) == '*') {
                         i++; regex.append(".*");
                     } else regex.append("[^/]*");
                     break;
                 default:
                     regex.append(c);
             }
